Z||000000||bHooper||05-20-2000||05:12 PM||||got everything back together and went for a spin around the block today.<P>however, every time i make a stearing correction, i get a metal on metal kind of scrapeing sound from the right rear.<P>any thoughts?<P>could this be the half shaft or is it more likely to be the rear rotor?<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||05-20-2000||05:23 PM||||I would make sure all the suspension components and steering column joint(under driver side dash is lubed up.)  Just a thought.  <P>Are you manifolds still glowing?||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||05-20-2000||06:46 PM||||Probably the ONE thing I haven't done yet, is lube.  However, I think I have a better idea about the scrape noise... I think it is the rear brake pads rubing agains the rotors.  I haven't turned the rotors, yet, or replaced the rear pads.  They do have a little oxidation on them.<P>The manifolds appear normal, now that the car is at a normal idle.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000003||WisconsinGT||05-20-2000||10:18 PM||||bHooper, you are correct! I had the same problem on my Fiero. Found out it was the brake pad rubbing. After a brake rebuild, no more noise. Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||05-20-2000||10:52 PM||||I'll bet it is your brake dust Shield! I had my back dis assembled and when I put it back together same noise. Since I had the brake dust shields painted I could see where the rotor rubbed against it. Coyote had same problem. After replace back suspension and brakes the brake dust shields rubbed. Just bend them in slightly.||||reg||4
Z||000005||2birds||05-20-2000||11:44 PM||||I have this only when I make a left steering correction, but mine sounds like it's from the right front, though.  I've checked the brake shields, replaced front wheel bearings, front calipers, pads, and hardware. to no relief.  Since it started when I lowered it, someone here suggested it may be the caliper being pulled into the rotor by the brake line (I have Goodridge braided hoses).  I haven't checked it yet, since I've been gone for a while, but it may be a place to check. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||05-21-2000||07:37 AM||||hmm, interesting.  I will check the shield to see if it is rubbing.<P>2birds, are you refering to the ebrake cable?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||bHooper||05-21-2000||05:30 PM||||A possible new twist to the "problem" arrose its ugly head today.<P>I checked the rotor, it did sping and did not appear to be rubbing the shield... even though there was a mild sound from the pads.<P>More importantly,after reinstalling the rear anti sway bar, I was torqueing the lug nuts when I noticed that the entire wheel/tire assembly, on the right rear, isnt set out as far as the driver side!  I was literaly rubbing the fender with my torque wrench, whereas the drivers sid has about 1/4-1/2 of a inch clearance!!!!!!<P><B>WTF?</B><P>Any ideas about this one?  I would think it would have to be a suspension problem, as the cradle bolted up properly (after a lot of convinceing).  You guys may remember, I had a difficult time getting the right rear strut to set into its bolting position in the engine compartment!  Do I have a bent A-arm? Or perhaps its not the right one?  (Pontiac had to fix that part of the suspension 10 years ago after I had a nasty little spin)<P>I took the car for a drive and it flet terribly out of align!  I cant remember if I had this feeling yesterday... I was just so anxiouse to go for a spin, nothing mattered.  However, today, I got about 8 laps around my neighborhood... so more time to notice such things.<P>I hope it's something that is fixable, but tolerable for a while, as I plan on haveing a custom suspension built sometime this summer.<P>TIA, for any help!!!!<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000008||2birds||05-21-2000||10:01 PM||||BHooper,<P>I was referring to the front brake hoses, but now that you mention it, when I had the rear caliper recall done 5 years ago, the right rear caliper was dragging a lot more than the left (about 10 times the running torque when spinning each side and the tranny in neutral), so the dealer replaced all of the e-brake cables under the recall.  It took a trip to Midas for a free estimate to get the dealer to do it, but it fixed the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000009||bHooper||05-21-2000||10:14 PM||||tommorowm I am going to disconect the anti sway bar and the e brake cables and see if that fix's my "problem".  if it does, i will reconect them so that i can have it inspected, then "fix" them afterwards!<P>any thoughts as to the "alignment" or offset problem of the rear wheel?<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||05-21-2000||10:38 PM||||After I put my suspension back together (I replaced everything and lowered my car) my alignment was whacked. Really whacked. The passenger side rear tire stuck out 1/4" but the driver side rear tire was in 1/4" I couldn't drive faster than 10 mph because my rears were so toed out they squealed. I had a major 4-wheel alignment done (took them 4 hours)and everything is normal. Tires are equally set even with the wheel well and no more toe problem. I suggest you take it to a good alignment shop with a good machine. They should be able to get it back into alignment with no problems.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||bHooper||05-22-2000||06:45 AM||||it just goes to show, that even if you are carefull and do things the proper way, that things still get screwed up.<P>Orief, your one post makes me feel a <B>whole</B> lot better abotu my suspension problem!<P>thanks<P>hoop<P>||||reg||1
